<http://vocabs.bcdh.uni-bonn.de/heritage_assets/silk> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <http://vocabs.bcdh.uni-bonn.de/heritage_assets/material/concept_a4bf16d5> ;
    skos:definition "A fine, lustrous, natural fiber obtained from the catepillar cocoons of silk moths, such as the domesticated Bombyx mori. Silkworm silk is composed of a heavy and light chain of core proteins called fibroins, which are coated by sericin, a collection of sticky, glue-like proteins.  Microscopically, raw silk appears as two strands that are held together with sericin protein. According to legend, silk was discovered by Chinese Empress Si-Ling-Shi when a cocoon fell in her tea. China maintained a monopoly on the production of silk fabric for almost 3,000 years.The worms were first cultivated in Japan about 195 CE and in Europe about 555 CE."@en ;
    skos:exactMatch <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300014072>,
        <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q37681>,
        <https://d-nb.info/gnd/4054289-0> ;
    skos:inScheme <http://vocabs.bcdh.uni-bonn.de/heritage_assets/material> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Seide"@de,
        "Silk"@en .

